WEBVTT

00:00:00.482 --> 00:00:03.322
Spark SQL and the DataFrame API are closely related.

00:00:03.322 --> 00:00:07.257
SQL expresses the logic with SQL syntax.

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:12.092
The DataFrame API expresses transformations through code.

00:00:12.092 --> 00:00:15.492
Both can use Spark's structured execution engine.

00:00:15.492 --> 00:00:21.797
For students who already know SQL, Spark SQL is a natural entry point into distributed processing.

00:00:21.797 --> 00:00:27.592
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:31.927
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:31.927 --> 00:00:35.752
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.752 --> 00:00:41.902
In a real setting such as a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions,

00:00:41.902 --> 00:00:47.517
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:52.117
before discussing syntax.

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:57.680
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
